Project goal

This project is a front-end validation prototype for a recipe memory and discovery experience. The goal is to help users find saved recipes by typing what they remember, such as an ingredient, a recipe type, or a general vibe.

The app is designed to feel like a natural search experience, even though it is not yet a full production app with a backend, real user data, or AI semantic search.

Current status

Status: Prototype / validation build

This version is a hardcoded demo using a static list of recipe data. It is not yet connected to a real backend or production database.

The current search is still limited to direct keyword matching and ingredient/title matching, but it is a strong foundation for validating the product concept.

What the prototype does

lets users type a search term
checks recipe titles and ingredient lists
filters relevant saved recipes
highlights the matching ingredient text
displays the recipe card with source and platform info
supports a browser-only demo flow for quick validation
Core user experience

The intended user behavior is:

user remembers an ingredient, a recipe vibe, or a rough idea
user types a natural-language search
the app returns relevant saved recipes
the user can click through to the original source
This is the main value proposition being validated.

What is already working

static data set is in place
search input is active
recipe cards render correctly
matching is case-insensitive
title and ingredient matches are detected
matching ingredients are visually highlighted
basic demo flow is ready for screen recording
What still needs improvement

The current search is not yet “human-like” enough for a strong demo.

It is still mostly literal keyword matching. It does not yet understand:

plural vs singular forms
synonym variations
category matching
fuzzy search input
results ranking by relevance
broader natural-language queries
Examples of things users may type:

banana
protein snack
healthy dessert
chocolate
yogurt
cheese pasta
sweet and easy
These should produce more relevant results than raw substring matching alone.

Why the prototype is still acceptable

This is a validation prototype, not the final product. That is the right scope for this stage.

A hardcoded prototype is appropriate when the goal is to answer:

does the concept feel valuable?
does the search experience make sense?
do users understand the product idea?
is this worth building further?
This project is intentionally lean to keep the focus on product validation instead of overbuilding infrastructure.

Known limitations

data is hardcoded
no backend or database
no real persistent user saved data
no real semantic search or AI matching yet
no PWA install behaviour yet
no share flow yet
no authentication or account system
This is expected at this phase.

What we are aiming to improve next

The next iteration should make the search feel more human without requiring a backend.

Planned improvements:

add singular/plural normalization
add synonym mapping for ingredients and recipe types
add tag and category metadata
score matching results by relevance
sort results from strongest match to weakest
improve empty states and polish
add demo queries for a more convincing LinkedIn video
This will make the prototype feel much closer to a real product without requiring the full stack.

Demo strategy

This version is intended for a short screen-recorded demo, not a full product walkthrough.

The video should show:

a user entering a rough search
relevant recipe cards appearing
ingredient highlights showing what matched
quick examples like banana, dessert, protein, yogurt, cheese pasta
This will make the concept instantly understandable without needing voiceover.

Validation goal

This prototype is meant to answer:

does the idea feel useful?
is the “search by memory” concept intuitive?
do users understand the product value quickly?
does the demo create curiosity and interest?
If the answer is yes, the next phase can include:

real data storage
smarter search layer
backend support
improved UX
installable app experience
share and save workflows
Conclusion

This is a lean concept validation prototype. It is not the final product, but it is already a useful proof of concept for the search experience and product direction.

The goal for this version is not to be complete. The goal is to show the idea clearly enough to gather feedback and validate whether it is worth continuing.

This is the right approach for a fast validation cycle.
